2.2 Keys in the goal display
============================

Goals arrive as trees in which every subterm is labelled, so point can
rest on any part of a goal and ask about it.

Key           Description                                                 Command
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘RET’         Press the control at point, or go to a message's position   ‘lean4-info-return’
‘M-.’         Jump to the definition of the subterm at point              ‘xref-find-definitions’
‘C-c C-t’     Jump to the definition of its type                          ‘lean4-info-goto-type-definition’
‘TAB’         Fold or unfold the trace or section at point                ‘lean4-info-toggle-fold’
‘C-c C-s’     Pin this position, or unpin the section point is in         ‘lean4-info-toggle-pin’
‘C-c C-SPC’   Pause or unpause the display                                ‘lean4-info-toggle-pause’
‘C-c C-g’     Bring a paused display up to date                           ‘lean4-info-refresh-paused’
‘C-c C-a’     Pause or unpause the file's message list                    ‘lean4-info-toggle-all-messages-pause’
‘C-c C-o’     Order that list by position or by nearness                  ‘lean4-info-toggle-message-order’

   Pinning a position keeps it on display as a section of its own, above
the section following point, and any number of positions can be pinned.
Each keeps updating: a pin is a marker, so it follows its declaration as
the file is edited, and it stays on display after moving to another
file.  Each pinned section has its own controls - one back to the
position, one to pause it, one to bring it up to date while paused, and
one to unpin it.  Pausing a pin is separate from pinning it: a pinned
goal follows its declaration through an edit unless told otherwise.
Pinning happens at point, so the followed section is left out until
point moves, rather than showing the same position twice; returning to a
pinned position later does show both.

   Every control has a key and a menu entry as well, so none of them
needs a mouse.  The menu entries are the same in the Lean menu and in
the goal display's own, since every one of these commands works from
either buffer; each says what pressing it will do next, so an item reads
"Unpin this position" where the position is already pinned.  With
several pins there is no one pin for a key to mean, so ‘C-c C-s’ means
the section point is standing in: inside a pinned section it unpins that
one, and anywhere else it pins or unpins the position point is on in the
Lean buffer.  ‘C-c C-SPC’ and ‘C-c C-g’ read the same way: inside a
pinned section they pause and refresh that section, anywhere else the
one following point.  ‘RET’ reaches the two things in the display that
lead anywhere: on a control it does what clicking it would, and on a
message heading it goes to the place the message was reported for.
Every control is therefore reachable without a mouse.

   The last two are bound here as well as in the Lean buffer, so the
display can be pinned or paused without leaving it.  Clicking their
controls does the same, and leaves the keyboard in the Lean buffer
rather than taking focus, as VS Code does.  Both states can hold at
once, and the heading says so: pinned means the display has stopped
following point, paused that it has stopped updating at all.

   Where there is nothing at point to report - outside a proof, say -
the display says ‘No info found.’ rather than going blank, which would
read like a display that had stopped working.  Messages from elsewhere
in the file are still listed below it.

   The type and docstring of the subterm at point are reported through
ElDoc, so they appear wherever ElDoc is configured to put them: the echo
area by default, or a child frame with a package such as ‘eldoc-box’.

   Traces fold like everything else, being sections themselves: ‘TAB’ or
the mouse, with the same indicator.  Lean does not send the children of
a trace node until something asks - a ‘simp’ trace on a real proof is
enormous - so opening one fetches them, once.


File: lean4-mode.info,  Node: The tactic state,  Next: How much of a goal to show,  Prev: Keys in the goal display,  Up: Usage

2.3 The tactic state
====================

The goals at point are listed under ‘Tactic state’, with the number of
them on the line below, both as VS Code words and places them.  A single
goal is counted too - ‘1 goal’ - so the line is there on every proof
rather than only on a branching one.

   A finished proof reads ‘goals accomplished’ instead, which is Lean's
own wording for it; VS Code says ‘No goals’ there and counts nothing,
and neither does this.

   The expected type at point follows, under ‘Expected type’, and is not
counted - VS Code counts only the tactic state.  Neither heading takes a
colon, as neither does in VS Code.


File: lean4-mode.info,  Node: How much of a goal to show,  Next: Errors and warnings,  Prev: The tactic state,  Up: Usage

2.4 How much of a goal to show
==============================

A goal in Mathlib can carry more hypotheses than fit on a screen, most
of them typeclass instances nobody is reasoning about.  Eight settings
decide how much of one is shown, the same eight VS Code gathers behind
the cog in its InfoView.  They are in the ‘Lean’ menu and the goal
display's own, under ‘How much of a goal to show’, as checkboxes; each
is also a command and an option.

Setting                                      Default      Command
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘lean4-info-show-goal-names’                 ‘t’          ‘lean4-info-toggle-goal-names’
‘lean4-info-emphasize-first-goal’            ‘nil’        ‘lean4-info-toggle-emphasize-first-goal’
‘lean4-info-target-first’                    ‘nil’        ‘lean4-info-toggle-target-first’
‘lean4-info-hide-type-assumptions’           ‘nil’        ‘lean4-info-toggle-type-assumptions’
‘lean4-info-hide-instance-assumptions’       ‘nil’        ‘lean4-info-toggle-instance-assumptions’
‘lean4-info-hide-inaccessible-assumptions’   ‘nil’        ‘lean4-info-toggle-inaccessible-assumptions’
‘lean4-info-hide-let-values’                 ‘nil’        ‘lean4-info-toggle-let-values’
‘lean4-info-expected-type-visibility’        ‘expanded’   ‘lean4-info-cycle-expected-type’

   Every default is VS Code's, so out of the box the whole goal is
shown.  The commands change a setting for the session; customizing it
makes the change stick.

   • Goal names are the ‘case inl’ labels a structured proof produces.
   • Emphasizing the first goal draws the others smaller and dimmer -
     the main goal is the one being worked on, the rest are context.
   • Showing the target first puts ‘⊢’ above the hypotheses, and
     reverses them, so that the target stays next to the hypothesis it
     was next to.
   • Type assumptions are hypotheses that are themselves types, such as
     the implicit ‘α : Type’ of a general lemma.  Instance assumptions
     are typeclass instances, ‘[Inhabited α]’ and its kind.  Hiding
     either drops the hypothesis entirely.
   • Inaccessible assumptions are the ones Lean prints with a dagger,
     ‘h✝’, which cannot be referred to by name.  They are dimmed rather
     than hidden by default.  Where a dagger name shares a line with a
     usable one, hiding takes away just the name.
   • Hiding let-values shows a ‘let’ binder's type but not its value,
     which on a large value is the difference between reading the goal
     and scrolling past it.
   • The expected type can be shown, shown folded, or left out.  The
     middle two are defaults rather than rules: the section still folds
     and unfolds with ‘TAB’, and a fold made by hand survives until the
     setting changes.

   Goals are kept as Lean sent them and rendered on the way into the
buffer, so changing one of these costs a redraw rather than another
request.  That is what lets a change reach a pinned or paused section,
where re-fetching would be precisely the wrong thing.  Goals from a
server too old for the interactive RPC arrive as text and cannot be
filtered; the settings simply have nothing to act on.


File: lean4-mode.info,  Node: Errors and warnings,  Next: Building dependencies,  Prev: How much of a goal to show,  Up: Usage

2.5 Errors and warnings
=======================

Diagnostics are reported through Flymake, which Eglot drives.  ‘C-c ! n’
and ‘C-c ! p’ move between them, and ‘flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics’
lists them.

   Not everything Lean sends is a problem.  It reports a completed
proof, trace output and whatever a project's own tactics emit at note
severity, all of which is meant for the goal display; flagging it in the
editor buries the errors among it.  So Flymake is shown errors and
warnings, and the rest goes to the goal display only.  Set
‘lean4-flymake-minimum-severity’ to ‘note’ to be shown everything, as VS
Code's Problems view does, or to ‘error’ for less.

   Messages appear in the goal display placed by Lean's own notion of
which declaration they belong to, so a message about a multi-line
declaration stays visible while point moves through it.  Terms inside a
message are as interactive as terms inside a goal.

   Those covering point are listed under ‘Messages’, set in under the
heading naming the position, along with the goals and the expected type.
Every message in the file is listed under ‘All messages’, at the outer
level, with two controls of its own: one orders the list by nearness to
point or by position in the file - nearness first by default, as in VS
Code, whose manual has All Messages "sorted by their proximity to the
text cursor by default" - and one holds the list as it is, separately
from the pause in the position heading.  While the display is paused a
third control appears in the position heading, between the way back to
the pinned position and the pin, which brings it up to date without
unpausing it.  Both headings count what is in them by severity, the way
VS Code's do - ‘All messages (1 ⊗ 5 ⓘ)’ for a file with one error and
five notes.

   Messages Lean marks as being for the goal display rather than the
editor - that is what ‘isSilent’ means, the completed-proof report among
them - are shown against the position they belong to and kept out of
both Flymake and ‘All messages’, as VS Code does with them.

   Lean emits three severities: error ‘⊗’, warning ‘⚠’ and information
‘ⓘ’.  LSP defines a fourth, hint, which this package draws as ‘ⓗ’, but
Lean's own ‘MessageSeverity’ has no such value and its server never
sends one.  The glyphs are picked for the frame, falling back to ‘E W I
H’ where none can be displayed.

   Each message is a section of its own, so a long one - a ‘simp’ trace,
a mismatch between two large terms - folds away without folding the
rest.  Sections are set in with spaces and their fold indicators drawn
by ‘magit-section’, in the fringe, the way a Magit buffer does both.
Its heading names the file, line and column, and carries an ‘↪’ control,
right-aligned, that sends point there.  Clicking works as ‘mouse-1’ does
throughout Emacs: on a control it acts, and anywhere else it moves point
- which is how a subterm is put under ElDoc, and how VS Code reads a
plain click too.  ‘RET’ does the same two things by keyboard, going to
the message's position from anywhere on its heading; the definition of a
subterm is ‘M-.’, wherever your xref frontend puts it.  Folding is
‘TAB’, and by mouse whatever ‘magit-section’ itself offers - a double
click, or a click on the fringe or margin, from version 4; nothing in
3.3.0 - so this display folds the way your Magit does.  The same control
appears in the top heading, but only while pinned: unpinned, the display
is following point and it would only lead where point already is.


